    <div class="prose-thai">
      <h2>The Pad Thai Problem</h2>
      <p>Pad Thai is arguably Thailand's most famous dish — and also one of the hardest to make keto. Traditional Pad Thai is built on rice noodles, which are almost pure carbohydrates. A typical restaurant serving contains 60-80g of carbs, mostly from the noodles and sugar in the sauce.</p>
      <p>But here's the good news: the soul of Pad Thai isn't the noodles — it's the sauce. That perfect balance of sweet, sour, salty, and umami can be achieved without sugar, and the noodles can be replaced with low-carb alternatives that actually work.</p>

      <h2>The Perfect Keto Pad Thai Sauce</h2>
      <p>This is the heart of the dish. Get this right, and your keto Pad Thai will taste 90% as good as the real thing.</p>
      <h3>Sauce Ingredients</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>3 tbsp fish sauce</li>
        <li>2 tbsp tamarind paste (about 3g net carbs total)</li>
        <li>1-2 tbsp erythritol or monk fruit sweetener</li>
        <li>1 tbsp rice vinegar</li>
        <li>1 tsp coconut aminos</li>
        <li>1/2 tsp chili flakes (or to taste)</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Sauce Instructions</h3>
      <p>Whisk all ingredients together until the sweetener dissolves. Taste and adjust — it should be tangy, salty, and slightly sweet. This makes enough sauce for 2 servings.</p>

      <h2>Noodle Alternatives Ranked</h2>
      <h3>1. Konjac/Shirataki Noodles (Best Overall)</h3>
      <p><strong>0g net carbs per serving.</strong> Konjac noodles are the closest thing to a miracle for keto Pad Thai. They have a neutral flavor, a satisfying chew, and zero net carbs. The key is preparation: rinse thoroughly, boil for 2-3 minutes, then dry-fry in a hot pan until all moisture is gone. This removes the characteristic odor and improves the texture dramatically.</p>

      <h3>2. Zucchini Noodles (Best Fresh Option)</h3>
      <p><strong>3g net carbs per cup.</strong> Zucchini noodles (zoodles) work surprisingly well in Pad Thai. They're fresh, light, and absorb the sauce nicely. Don't overcook them — 1-2 minutes in the pan is enough. Spiralize them thin for the best texture.</p>

      <h3>3. Hearts of Palm Noodles (Best Texture)</h3>
      <p><strong>2g net carbs per cup.</strong> Hearts of palm noodles have a firm, almost al dente texture that's closer to real pasta than any other low-carb option. They're slightly more expensive but worth it for special occasions.</p>

      <h3>4. Cabbage "Noodles" (Budget Pick)</h3>
      <p><strong>3g net carbs per cup.</strong> Thinly sliced green cabbage works in a pinch. It won't fool anyone into thinking it's pasta, but it adds bulk and crunch. Best mixed with another noodle alternative.</p>

      <h2>Keto Pad Thai Recipe</h2>
      <h3>Pad Thai Ingredients (Serves 2)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>2 packs konjac noodles (or 2 cups zucchini noodles)</li>
        <li>8 oz shrimp or chicken, peeled/deveined if using shrimp</li>
        <li>2 eggs</li>
        <li>1 cup bean sprouts</li>
        <li>2 green onions, sliced</li>
        <li>2 tbsp crushed peanuts</li>
        <li>Lime wedges, cilantro, and chili flakes for garnish</li>
        <li>1 batch keto Pad Thai sauce (above)</li>
        <li>2 tbsp avocado oil or coconut oil</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Pad Thai Instructions</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Prepare noodles: Rinse konjac noodles, boil 2-3 minutes, drain, and dry-fry in a hot pan for 3-4 minutes until all moisture evaporates. Set aside.</li>
        <li>Heat oil in a wok or large skillet over high heat.</li>
        <li>Cook protein: Add shrimp or chicken and stir-fry until just cooked (2-3 minutes for shrimp, 4-5 for chicken). Push to the side.</li>
        <li>Scramble eggs: Crack eggs into the pan and scramble quickly. Mix with the protein.</li>
        <li>Add noodles and sauce: Toss in the prepared noodles and pour the sauce over everything. Stir-fry for 1-2 minutes until well combined.</li>
        <li>Add bean sprouts and green onions: Toss for 30 seconds — you want the sprouts to stay crunchy.</li>
        <li>Serve immediately: Top with crushed peanuts, cilantro, chili flakes, and a lime wedge.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Macros per serving:</strong> ~380 calories, 24g fat, 6g net carbs, 32g protein</p>
